"Compares predicted probabilities with observed outcome frequencies to assess whether probability statements match reality.\n\nUse when: A model’s probabilities will support decisions and need reliability assessment. A held-out dataset contains enough outcomes for meaningful grouped comparisons.\n\nAvoid when: Bins contain too few cases or evaluation data were reused to fit the calibrator.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes mean predicted probability within a bin. Vertical position encodes observed positive frequency in that bin, compared with the equality diagonal.\n\nBuild: Choose and disclose probability bins or a justified smooth calibration estimator. Calculate bin counts, mean predictions, and observed frequencies using held-out records. Plot the results on 0–1 axes with uncertainty and a separate prediction-distribution display if useful.\n\nCheck: Check each point’s coordinates and outcome denominator use the same records. Verify bin counts or uncertainty reveal sparse regions. State whether predictions were calibrated on separate data.\n\nPitfalls: A well-calibrated model can still have weak discrimination. Bin choice can hide local errors; inspect alternate reasonable bins rather than presenting one arbitrary grouping as definitive.\n\nExample: Assess a synthetic failure-probability model using held-out predictions, ten declared bins, and visible counts for every probability group.\n\nAlternatives: ROC curve, Precision-recall curve, P–P plot."
